  28A1'   00                    	DB	0
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-126


                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  28A2'                         EXADDR:
  28A2'   AF                    	XRA	A
  28A3'   32 2EF0'              	STA	SRNGF
  28A6'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  28A7'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  28A9'   CA 28D2'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  28AC'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  28AD'   CD 28E7'              	CALL	EXNUM
  28B0'   DA 28D0'              	JC	EXA_NO
  28B3'   22 2EF1'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  28B6'   22 2EF3'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  28B9'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  28BA'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  28BC'   C2 28C9'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  28BF'   13                    	INX	D
  28C0'   CD 28E7'              	CALL	EXNUM
  28C3'   DA 28D0'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  28C6'   22 2EF3'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  28C9'                         EXA_YS:
  28C9'   EB                    	XCHG
  28CA'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  28CC'   32 2EF0'              	STA	SRNGF
  28CF'   C9                    	RET
  28D0'                         EXA_NO:
  28D0'   EB                    	XCHG
  28D1'   C9                    	RET
  28D2'                         EXA_AL:
  28D2'   23                    	INX	H
  28D3'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  28D4'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  28D7'   22 2EF1'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  28DA'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  28DD'   22 2EF3'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  28E0'   E1                    	POP	H
  28E1'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  28E3'   32 2EF0'              	STA	SRNGF
  28E6'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  28E7'                         EXNUM:
  28E7'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  28E8'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  28EA'   D8                    	RC
  28EB'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  28ED'   3F                    	CMC
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-127


  28EE'   D8                    	RC
  28EF'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  28F2'                         EXN_LP:
  28F2'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  28F3'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  28F5'   DA 290F'              	JC	EXN_DN
  28F8'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  28FA'   D2 290F'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  28FD'   13                    	INX	D
  28FE'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  28FF'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  2900'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  2902'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2903'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  2904'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  2905'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  2906'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  2907'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  2908'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  2909'   D1                    	POP	D
  290A'   09                    	DAD	B
  290B'   C1                    	POP	B
  290C'   C3 28F2'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  290F'                         EXN_DN:
  290F'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  2910'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  2911'                         EX_S:
  2911'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2912'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2913'   CA 2955'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  2916'   32 2EF5'              	STA	SDELIM
  2919'   23                    	INX	H
  291A'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  291D'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2920'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  2921'   CD 295B'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2924'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2925'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2926'   CA 2955'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  2929'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  292C'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  292E'   32 2EBD'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  2931'   11 2F07'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  2934'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  2936'   CD 295B'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2939'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  293A'   32 2F06'              	STA	SREPLN
  293D'   AF                    	XRA	A
  293E'   32 2EF6'              	STA	SGFLAG
  2941'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2942'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  2944'   C2 294D'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  2947'   23                    	INX	H
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-128


  2948'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  294A'   32 2EF6'              	STA	SGFLAG
  294D'                         EX_SG:
  294D'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  294E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  294F'   C2 26DC'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  2952'   C3 297B'              	JMP	SUBGO
  2955'                         SUBNOP:
  2955'   21 27A4'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2958'   C3 26EA'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  295B'                         SCOPY:
  295B'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  295D'                         SCP_LP:
  295D'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  295E'   B9                    	CMP	C
  295F'   D2 2978'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  2962'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2963'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2964'   CA 2978'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  2967'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2968'   21 2EF5'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  296B'   BE                    	CMP	M
  296C'   E1                    	POP	H
  296D'   CA 2977'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  2970'   12                    	STAX	D
  2971'   13                    	INX	D
  2972'   23                    	INX	H
  2973'   04                    	INR	B
  2974'   C3 295D'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  2977'                         SCP_SK:
  2977'   23                    	INX	H
  2978'                         SCP_DN:
  2978'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2979'   12                    	STAX	D
  297A'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  297B'                         SUBGO:
  297B'   CD 12F8'              	CALL	GZONE
  297E'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  2981'   22 2F04'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  2984'   21 2F01'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  2987'   CD 0429'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  298A'   AF                    	XRA	A
  298B'   32 2EF7'              	STA	SANY
  298E'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2991'   22 2EF9'              	SHLD	SBACK
  2994'   3A 2EF0'              	LDA	SRNGF
  2997'   B7                    	ORA	A
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-129


  2998'   CA 29AA'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  299B'   CD 226A'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  299E'   DA 2A26'              	JC	SUBBAD
  29A1'   2A 2EF1'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  29A4'   CD 1266'              	CALL	GOLN
  29A7'   C3 29B0'              	JMP	SUBLP
  29AA'                         SUB_1L:
  29AA'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  29AD'   22 2EFB'              	SHLD	SLINES
  29B0'                         SUBLP:
  29B0'   CD 2A2C'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  29B3'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29B4'   CA 29C2'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  29B7'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  29B9'   32 2EF7'              	STA	SANY
  29BC'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  29BF'   22 2EF9'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  29C2'                         SUB_NX:
  29C2'   2A 2EFB'              	LHLD	SLINES
  29C5'   2B                    	DCX	H
  29C6'   22 2EFB'              	SHLD	SLINES
  29C9'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  29CA'   B5                    	ORA	L
  29CB'   CA 29E9'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  29CE'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  29D1'   AF                    	XRA	A
  29D2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  29D5'   3A 2EF7'              	LDA	SANY
  29D8'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29D9'   CA 29E3'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  29DC'   2A 2EF9'              	LHLD	SBACK
  29DF'   23                    	INX	H
  29E0'   22 2EF9'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  29E3'                         SUB_AE:
  29E3'   CD 0B02'              	CALL	ATEOF
  29E6'   D2 29B0'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  29E9'                         SUBDON:
  29E9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  29EC'   3A 2EF7'              	LDA	SANY
  29EF'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29F0'   CA 2A0E'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  29F3'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  29F5'   32 2E31'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  29F8'   2A 2EF9'              	LHLD	SBACK
  29FB'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  29FC'   B5                    	ORA	L
  29FD'   CA 2A08'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  2A00'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  2A02'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  2A05'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-130


  2A08'                         SUB_PL:
  2A08'   CD 1310'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  2A0B'   C3 085D'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  2A0E'                         SUBNFD:
  2A0E'   21 2F01'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  2A11'   CD 03D0'              	CALL	BACKTO
  2A14'   2A 2F04'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  2A17'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  2A1A'   21 2774'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2A1D'   CD 26EA'              	CALL	EXERR
  2A20'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2A23'   C3 26E5'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  2A26'                         SUBBAD:
  2A26'   21 2762'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  2A29'   C3 26DF'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  2A2C'                         SUBLIN:
  2A2C'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2A2D'   32 2EF8'              	STA	SLCHG
  2A30'   CD 2AAB'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2A33'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  2A36'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  2A36'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  2A39'   DA 2A41'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  2A3C'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  2A3E'   C2 2A36'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  2A41'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  2A41'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2A44'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2A45'   CD 18EB'              	CALL	CEND
  2A48'   D1                    	POP	D
  2A49'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  2A4C'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  2A4D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  2A50'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  2A51'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  2A52'   FA 2AA4'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-131


  2A55'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  2A56'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  2A57'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2A5A'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  2A5D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  2A60'   DA 2AA4'              	JC	SUBL_D
  2A63'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  2A66'   EB                    	XCHG
  2A67'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  2A6A'   EB                    	XCHG
  2A6B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  2A6E'   EB                    	XCHG
  2A6F'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  2A70'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  2A73'   D1                    	POP	D
  2A74'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  2A75'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  2A78'   3A 2EF7'              	LDA	SANY
  2A7B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A7C'   C2 2A89'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2A7F'   3A 2EF8'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2A82'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A83'   C2 2A89'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2A86'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  2A89'                         SUBL_C:
  2A89'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  2A8C'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2A8D'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  2A8F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  2A92'   11 2F06'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  2A95'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  2A98'                         SUBL_I:
  2A98'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A9A'   32 2EF8'              	STA	SLCHG
  2A9D'   3A 2EF6'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  2AA0'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2AA1'   C2 2A41'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  2AA4'                         SUBL_D:
  2AA4'   CD 2AAB'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2AA7'   3A 2EF8'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2AAA'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  2AAB'                         SUBBOL:
  2AAB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2AAE'   CD 063D'              	CALL	BOL
  2AB1'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-132


  2AB4'                         VC_SRCH:
  2AB4'   CD 0820'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2AB7'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2AB8'   CD 074B'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2ABB'   22 2EE6'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2ABE'   C1                    	POP	B
  2ABF'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2AC0'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2AC2'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  2AC4'   CA 2AC9'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  2AC7'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  2AC9'                         VS_DIR:
  2AC9'   32 2EBB'              	STA	SDIR
  2ACC'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  2ACF'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2AD2'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2AD3'   CD 2013'              	CALL	RDLINE
  2AD6'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2AD7'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  2AD8'   3A 2E92'              	LDA	RLLEN
  2ADB'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2ADC'   CA 2AF1'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  2ADF'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  2AE0'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  2AE2'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2AE5'   19                    	DAD	D
  2AE6'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  2AE8'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  2AE9'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  2AEC'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2AEE'   32 2EBD'              	STA	SHAVE
  2AF1'                         VS_OLD:
  2AF1'   3A 2EBD'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2AF4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2AF5'   CA 2B01'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  2AF8'   3A 2EBB'              	LDA	SDIR
  2AFB'   32 2EBC'              	STA	SDIRE
  2AFE'   C3 2B2A'              	JMP	SRCHN
  2B01'                         VS_NOP:
  2B01'   21 27A4'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2B04'   C3 26EA'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  2B07'                         VC_N:
  2B07'   CD 0820'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2B0A'   3A 2EBB'              	LDA	SDIR
  2B0D'   C3 2B17'              	JMP	VN_GO
  2B10'                         VCB_N:
  2B10'   CD 0820'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2B13'   3A 2EBB'              	LDA	SDIR
  2B16'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  2B17'                         VN_GO:
  2B17'   32 2EBC'              	STA	SDIRE
  2B1A'   CD 074B'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2B1D'   22 2EE6'              	SHLD	SCNT
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-133


  2B20'   3A 2EBD'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2B23'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B24'   CA 2B01'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  2B27'   C3 2B2A'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  2B2A'                         SRCHN:
  2B2A'   CD 12F8'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  2B2D'   CD 0774'              	CALL	BRKON		; where it started (the sweep moves the cursor
                                				;   with PUTCUR, which does not maintain LNDLT;
                                				;   GOTO does), and an ESC may stop it
  2B30'                         SN_LP:
  2B30'   CD 2B65'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  2B33'   DA 2B50'              	JC	SN_BAD
  2B36'   2A 2EE6'              	LHLD	SCNT
  2B39'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2B3A'   22 2EE6'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2B3D'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2B3E'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2B3F'   C2 2B30'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  2B42'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  2B45'   CD 07B1'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2B48'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2B4A'   32 2EC8'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  2B4D'   C3 1310'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  2B50'                         SN_BAD:
  2B50'   CD 07B1'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2B53'   DA 07C9'              	JC	BRKBAK		; stopped, which is not the same as not found
  2B56'   CD 07BC'              	CALL	RSTPL		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  2B59'   21 2774'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2B5C'   CD 26EA'              	CALL	EXERR
  2B5F'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2B62'   C3 26E5'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  2B65'                         SRCHGO:
  2B65'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2B67'   32 2EEF'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  2B6A'   CD 2B80'              	CALL	SRCHW
  2B6D'   D0                    	RNC
  2B6E'   CD 07B5'              	CALL	BRKQ
  2B71'   D8                    	RC			; stopped: no second pass
  2B72'   3A 2EBC'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2B75'   2F                    	CMA
  2B76'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  2B79'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2B7A'   32 2EEF'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  2B7D'   C3 2B80'              	JMP	SRCHW
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-134


                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  2B80'                         SRCHW:
  2B80'   3A 2EBC'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2B83'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2B86'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2B89'   22 2E2E'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  2B8C'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2B8D'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  2B90'                         SW_LP:
  2B90'   CD 07B5'              	CALL	BRKQ		; an ESC spent the count, here or on the way to
  2B93'   D8                    	RC			;   the wrapped pass: SCRLST no longer pages
  2B94'   CD 2BA0'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2B97'   D0                    	RNC
  2B98'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  2B9B'   D2 2B90'              	JNC	SW_LP
  2B9E'   37                    	STC
  2B9F'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  2BA0'                         SRCH1:
  2BA0'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2BA3'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  2BA6'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BA7'   FA 2BCB'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2BAA'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  2BAD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2BB0'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  2BB3'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  2BB4'   3A 2EEF'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2BB7'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BB8'   CA 2BC1'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  2BBB'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2BBC'   B1                    	ORA	C
  2BBD'   37                    	STC
  2BBE'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2BBF'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2BC0'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  2BC1'                         SR1_FW:
  2BC1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  2BC4'   D8                    	RC
  2BC5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  2BC8'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  2BCB'                         SR1_BK:
  2BCB'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-135


  2BCE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2BD1'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  2BD4'   D8                    	RC
  2BD5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  2BD8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  2BDB'   D8                    	RC
  2BDC'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  2BDF'                         MSGNAM:
  2BDF'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2BE0'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2BE2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  2BE5'   E1                    	POP	H
  2BE6'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  2BE7'   23                    	INX	H
  2BE8'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2BE9'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2BEB'   C2 2BF8'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  2BEE'   21 2812'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  2BF1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2BF4'   E1                    	POP	H
  2BF5'   C3 2C42'              	JMP	MN_END
  2BF8'                         MN_HAS:
  2BF8'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  2BFA'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  2BFD'   E1                    	POP	H
  2BFE'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2BFF'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  2C00'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2C01'   CA 2C0F'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  2C04'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2C05'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  2C07'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2C0A'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  2C0C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-136


  2C0F'                         MN_NAM:
  2C0F'   E1                    	POP	H
  2C10'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2C11'   23                    	INX	H
  2C12'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  2C14'                         MN_NM:
  2C14'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2C15'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2C17'   CA 2C22'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  2C1A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2C1D'   23                    	INX	H
  2C1E'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2C1F'   C2 2C14'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  2C22'                         MN_EXT:
  2C22'   E1                    	POP	H
  2C23'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  2C26'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  2C27'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2C28'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2C2A'   CA 2C42'              	JZ	MN_END
  2C2D'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  2C2F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2C32'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  2C34'                         MN_XL:
  2C34'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2C35'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2C37'   CA 2C42'              	JZ	MN_END
  2C3A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2C3D'   23                    	INX	H
  2C3E'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2C3F'   C2 2C34'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  2C42'                         MN_END:
  2C42'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2C44'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  2C47'                         MSGFLG:
  2C47'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  2C49'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C4A'   CA 2C5A'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2C4D'   3A 2E31'              	LDA	MODF
  2C50'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C51'   CA 2C5A'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2C54'   21 281C'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  2C57'   CD 2C6F'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2C5A'                         MF_RO:
  2C5A'   3A 2EA8'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  2C5D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C5E'   CA 2C67'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  2C61'   21 2827'              	LXI	H,SROM
  2C64'   CD 2C6F'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2C67'                         MF_NEW:
  2C67'   3A 2EA6'              	LDA	MSGNEW
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-137


  2C6A'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C6B'   C8                    	RZ
  2C6C'   21 2832'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  2C6F'                         MF_PUT:
  2C6F'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2C70'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C71'   C2 2C7D'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  2C74'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2C76'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2C77'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  2C79'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2C7C'   E1                    	POP	H
  2C7D'                         MF_STR:
  2C7D'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  2C80'                         EMITW:
  2C80'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2C81'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2C82'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2C84'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2C87'   CD 2CA5'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2C8A'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  2C8D'   CD 2CA5'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2C90'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  2C93'   CD 2CA5'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2C96'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2C99'   CD 2CA5'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2C9C'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  2C9D'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2C9F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2CA2'   D1                    	POP	D
  2CA3'   C1                    	POP	B
  2CA4'   C9                    	RET
  2CA5'                         EWDIG:
  2CA5'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2CA7'                         EW_LP:
  2CA7'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2CA8'   93                    	SUB	E
  2CA9'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2CAA'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2CAB'   9A                    	SBB	D
  2CAC'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  2CAD'   DA 2CB4'              	JC	EW_DN
  2CB0'   0C                    	INR	C
  2CB1'   C3 2CA7'              	JMP	EW_LP
  2CB4'                         EW_DN:
  2CB4'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  2CB5'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2CB6'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2CB7'   C2 2CBD'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  2CBA'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2CBB'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2CBC'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-138


  2CBD'                         EW_PR:
  2CBD'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2CBE'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2CC0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2CC3'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2CC5'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2CC6'                         ISEMPT:
  2CC6'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2CC9'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2CCA'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2CCB'   C0                    	RNZ
  2CCC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  2CCF'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2CD0'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2CD1'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  2CD2'                         MSGCTG:
  2CD2'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2CD5'   CD 2BDF'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2CD8'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  2CDA'   CD 2C47'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  2CDD'   CD 2CC6'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  2CE0'   C2 2CE9'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  2CE3'   21 2845'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  2CE6'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  2CE9'                         MG_POS:
  2CE9'   21 2838'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  2CEC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2CEF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  2CF2'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  2CF3'   CD 2C80'              	CALL	EMITW
  2CF6'   21 283F'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  2CF9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2CFC'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  2CFF'   23                    	INX	H
  2D00'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2D01'   CD 2C80'              	CALL	EMITW
  2D04'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  2D07'   23                    	INX	H
  2D08'   D1                    	POP	D
  2D09'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  2D0C'   C8                    	RZ
  2D0D'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2D0E'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  2D10'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2D13'   E1                    	POP	H
  2D14'   C3 2C80'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-139


                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  2D17'                         FILMSG:
  2D17'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  2D1A'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2D1C'   C8                    	RZ
  2D1D'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2D20'   CD 2BDF'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2D23'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  2D24'   C3 2C47'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  2D27'                         WRMSG:
  2D27'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  2D2A'   CD 2BDF'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2D2D'   21 2857'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  2D30'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  2D33'                         MSGPST:
  2D33'   3A 2EA5'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  2D36'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2D37'   C8                    	RZ
  2D38'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2D39'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2D3A'   32 2EA5'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2D3D'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2D3E'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  2D40'   CA 2D5C'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  2D43'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  2D45'   CA 2D62'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  2D48'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
  2D4A'   CA 2D53'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  2D4D'   CD 2CD2'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  2D50'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2D53'                         MP_BIG:
  2D53'   21 27F3'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  2D56'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  2D59'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2D5C'                         MP_FIL:
  2D5C'   CD 2D17'              	CALL	FILMSG
  2D5F'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2D62'                         MP_WRT:
  2D62'   CD 2D27'              	CALL	WRMSG
  2D65'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  2D68'                         VC_CTLG:
  2D68'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  2D6A'   32 2EA5'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2D6D'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  2D6F'   32 2E32'              	STA	RINTENT
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-140


  2D72'   C3 074B'              	JMP	GETCNT		; a count typed before it is spent here, not
                                				;   left for the next command
                                
  2D75'                         CMDTAB:
  2D75'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
  2D76'   2D68'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
  2D78'   68                    	DB	'h'
  2D79'   0898'                 	DW	VC_H
  2D7B'   6C                    	DB	'l'
  2D7C'   08C2'                 	DW	VC_L
  2D7E'   6A                    	DB	'j'
  2D7F'   0993'                 	DW	VC_J
  2D81'   6B                    	DB	'k'
  2D82'   09A5'                 	DW	VC_K
  2D84'   30                    	DB	'0'
  2D85'   08F5'                 	DW	VC_0
  2D87'   5E                    	DB	'^'
  2D88'   0913'                 	DW	VC_CRT
  2D8A'   24                    	DB	'$'
  2D8B'   0951'                 	DW	VC_EOL
  2D8D'   0D                    	DB	CR			; <CR> -- down to the first non-blank
  2D8E'   0975'                 	DW	VC_CR
  2D90'   77                    	DB	'w'
  2D91'   0B84'                 	DW	VC_W
  2D93'   62                    	DB	'b'
  2D94'   0BD5'                 	DW	VC_B
  2D96'   65                    	DB	'e'
  2D97'   0D6B'                 	DW	VC_E
  2D99'   57                    	DB	'W'
  2D9A'   0B8B'                 	DW	VCBGW
  2D9C'   42                    	DB	'B'
  2D9D'   0BDC'                 	DW	VCBGB
  2D9F'   45                    	DB	'E'
  2DA0'   0D72'                 	DW	VCBGE
  2DA2'   47                    	DB	'G'
  2DA3'   1236'                 	DW	VC_G
  2DA5'   67                    	DB	'g'
  2DA6'   1289'                 	DW	VC_LG
  2DA8'   78                    	DB	'x'
  2DA9'   1880'                 	DW	VC_X
  2DAB'   5A                    	DB	'Z'
  2DAC'   2086'                 	DW	VC_BZ
  2DAE'   66                    	DB	'f'
  2DAF'   021A'                 	DW	VC_FF
  2DB1'   46                    	DB	'F'
  2DB2'   021F'                 	DW	VC_FB
  2DB4'   74                    	DB	't'
  2DB5'   0224'                 	DW	VC_TF
  2DB7'   54                    	DB	'T'
  2DB8'   0229'                 	DW	VC_TB
  2DBA'   3B                    	DB	';'
  2DBB'   0240'                 	DW	VC_SEMI
  2DBD'   2C                    	DB	','
  2DBE'   024A'                 	DW	VC_COMMA
  2DC0'   79                    	DB	'y'
  2DC1'   1B90'                 	DW	VC_Y
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-141


  2DC3'   59                    	DB	'Y'
  2DC4'   1B98'                 	DW	VY_YY
  2DC6'   70                    	DB	'p'
  2DC7'   1DCE'                 	DW	VC_P
  2DC9'   50                    	DB	'P'
  2DCA'   1DE3'                 	DW	VCB_P
  2DCC'   69                    	DB	'i'
  2DCD'   190C'                 	DW	VC_I
  2DCF'   61                    	DB	'a'
  2DD0'   1912'                 	DW	VC_A
  2DD2'   41                    	DB	'A'
  2DD3'   1932'                 	DW	VCB_A
  2DD5'   49                    	DB	'I'
  2DD6'   194E'                 	DW	VCB_I
  2DD8'   72                    	DB	'r'
  2DD9'   1A90'                 	DW	VC_R
  2DDB'   52                    	DB	'R'
  2DDC'   1AE9'                 	DW	VCB_R
  2DDE'   64                    	DB	'd'
  2DDF'   1AFE'                 	DW	VC_D
  2DE1'   44                    	DB	'D'
  2DE2'   1865'                 	DW	VCB_D
  2DE4'   43                    	DB	'C'
  2DE5'   185F'                 	DW	VCB_C
  2DE7'   63                    	DB	'c'
  2DE8'   1B06'                 	DW	VC_C
  2DEA'   6F                    	DB	'o'
  2DEB'   199C'                 	DW	VC_O
  2DED'   4F                    	DB	'O'
  2DEE'   19B1'                 	DW	VCB_O
  2DF0'   4A                    	DB	'J'
  2DF1'   19CB'                 	DW	VCB_J
  2DF3'   7E                    	DB	'~'
  2DF4'   1A4D'                 	DW	VC_TILD
  2DF6'   3A                    	DB	':'
  2DF7'   20B1'                 	DW	VC_EX
  2DF9'   2F                    	DB	'/'
  2DFA'   2AB4'                 	DW	VC_SRCH
  2DFC'   3F                    	DB	'?'
  2DFD'   2AB4'                 	DW	VC_SRCH
  2DFF'   6E                    	DB	'n'
  2E00'   2B07'                 	DW	VC_N
  2E02'   4E                    	DB	'N'
  2E03'   2B10'                 	DW	VCB_N
  2E05'   48                    	DB	'H'
  2E06'   0E2F'                 	DW	VC_HIGH
  2E08'   4D                    	DB	'M'
  2E09'   0E46'                 	DW	VC_MIDL
  2E0B'   4C                    	DB	'L'
  2E0C'   0E61'                 	DW	VC_LOWL
  2E0E'   06                    	DB	06H			; ^F -- page forward
  2E0F'   0E7E'                 	DW	VC_PGDN
  2E11'   02                    	DB	02H			; ^B -- page back
  2E12'   0EDC'                 	DW	VC_PGUP
  2E14'   04                    	DB	04H			; ^D -- half page down
  2E15'   0FBD'                 	DW	VC_HFDN
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-142


  2E17'   15                    	DB	15H			; ^U -- half page up
  2E18'   1084'                 	DW	VC_HFUP
  2E1A'   0C                    	DB	0CH			; ^L -- redraw
  2E1B'   1233'                 	DW	VC_REDR
  2E1D'   2E                    	DB	'.'			; '.' -- repeat the last change
  2E1E'   017D'                 	DW	VC_DOT
  2E20'   75                    	DB	'u'			; 'u' -- undo (and undo the undo)
  2E21'   0545'                 	DW	VC_UNDO
  2E23'   6D                    	DB	'm'		; m{a-c} -- set a mark
  2E24'   032E'                 	DW	VC_MK
  2E26'   60                    	DB	60H		; `{a-c} -- back to its exact spot
  2E27'   0333'                 	DW	VC_BQ
  2E29'   27                    	DB	27H		; '{a-c} -- back to its line's first non-blank
  2E2A'   0338'                 	DW	VC_AQ
  2E2C'   00                    	DB	0
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DATA
                                ;============================================================
  2E2D'   00                    EDMODE: DB	EM_CMD
  2E2E'   0000                  CMDCNT: DW	0		; count prefix (0 = none)
  2E30'   00                    QUITF:	DB	0		; set 1 by :q / :wq
  2E31'   00                    MODF:	DB	0		; 1 = unsaved changes
  2E32'   00                    RINTENT:DB	R_FULL		; render intent for the last key (SCRN.MAC reads)
  2E33'   01                    ILN:	DB	1		; lines an R_ILIN paint opens (SCRN.MAC reads)
  2E34'   00                    ICHAPP:	DB	0		; 1 = the R_ICH char went at the row's end, so
                                				;   nothing follows it to shift: no ICH needed
  2E35'   01                    DCHN:	DB	1		; the cells an R_DCH paint deletes (SCRN.MAC reads)
  2E36'   00                    DCHBS:	DB	0		; 1 = they went left of the cursor (insert's BS)
  2E37'   00                    LNALL:	DB	0		; 1 = this R_LINE is drawn from the line's first
                                				;   row, not the cursor's (SCRN.MAC clears it)
  2E38'   00                    ILABOV:	DB	0		; 1 = the row above an R_ILIN's opened rows lost
                                				;   text to them and must be redrawn too
  2E39'   FFFE                  GOALCOL:DW	0FFFEH		; sticky goal screen column for j/k (0FFFFH = EOL, 0FFFEH = stale)
  2E3B'   00                    WBIG:	DB	0		; word motion: 0 = w/b/e, 1 = W/B/E
  2E3C'   00                    WPULL:	DB	0		; w/W ended off a line's end and was pulled back
  2E3D'   00                    HFAMT:	DB	0		; ^D/^U: rows (vim's 'scroll'); 0 = NEDIT/2
  2E3E'   00                    HFN:	DB	0		; ^D/^U: the rows this one goes
  2E3F'   0000                  HFTP:	DW	0		; ^D/^U: -> the new top line's entry
  2E41'   00                    HTN:	DB	0		; row table: its entries
  2E42'   00                    HTCUR:	DB	0		; ... the cursor line's
  2E43'   00                    HTTP:	DB	0		; ... the top line's
  2E44'   00                    HTEOF:	DB	0		; ... 1 = its last is the file's last line
  2E45'   00                    HTBOF:	DB	0		; ... 1 = its first is the file's first
  2E46'   0000                  RWR:	DW	0		; ^F/^B: rows still to go
  2E48'   00                    RWMV:	DB	0		; ^F/^B: 1 = the table was made again on the way
  2E49'   00                    OVDIR:	DB	0		; OVLAP: 0 = h2.. are above h1 in the table
  2E4A'   0000                  JKCNT:	DW	0		; j/k: count
  2E4C'   0000                  JKN:	DW	0		; j/k: lines scanned for (min(count, limit))
  2E4E'   0000                  JKMOV:	DW	0		; j: lines moved
  2E50'   0000                  JKBK:	DW	0		; k: scan position, bytes back from GAPBEG
  2E52'   00                    JKPHN:	DB	0		; j: 1 = moved may be one less (last LF ends the file)
  2E53'   00                    JKLST:	DB	0		; j: last byte of the scan
  2E54'   0000                  JKVC:	DW	0		; j/k landing: screen column
  2E56'   0000                  JKLS:	DW	0		; j/k landing: last content char (0FFFFH = none)
  0001                          PG	EQU	1		; pending-prefix codes held in GPEND/GSAVE
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-143


  0002                          PZ	EQU	2
  0003                          PR	EQU	3		;   ('r', whose next key is its operand)
  0004                          PM	EQU	4		; which of 'm' / '`' / ''' is running (MKWHAT, not
  0005                          PJ	EQU	5		;   GPEND: each reads its own mark letter, so none
  0006                          PJL	EQU	6		;   of them leaves anything pending)
                                ;--- '.' (repeat): see the '.' section above for how these are driven.
  2E58'   00                    DOTCHG: DB	0		; 1 = the command being typed changed the text
  2E59'   01                    DOTSTA: DB	1		; 1 = the next key starts a new recording
  2E5A'   00                    DOTRPL: DB	0		; 1 = '.' is replaying, so nothing is recorded
  2E5B'   00                    DOTOVF: DB	0		; 1 = the change outgrew the scratch
  2E5C'   00                    DOTLK:	DB	0		; the last key recorded, encoded
  2E5D'   0000*                 DOTSP:	DW	DOTSCR		; the scratch's write pointer
  2E5F'   00                    DOTLEN: DB	0		; keys in DOTBUF (0 = nothing to repeat)
  2E60'   00                    DOTIX:	DB	0		; the replay's read index into DOTBUF
  2E61'   0000                  DOTNCT: DW	0		; the count typed on the '.' (0 = none)
                                
  2E63'   00                    GPEND:	DB	0		; pending 'g' / 'Z' / 'r' (gg / ZZ / r{char})
  2E64'   00                    GSAVE:	DB	0		; the pending snapshot for the current command
  2E65'   00                    PENDOP: DB	0		; pending operator ('d' for dd)
  2E66'   00                    PSAVE:	DB	0		; pending-op snapshot for the current command
  2E67'   00                    DDSNG:	DB	0		; 1 = the current dd deletes a single line (DL paint)
  2E68'   00                    OPCLSV: DB	0		; operator: motion class (0 excl, 1 incl, 2 linewise,
                                				;   3 excl but a jump -- no end-of-line clamp)
  2E69'   00                    CWEOL:	DB	0		; operator: the motion is 'cw' rewritten to 'ce',
                                				;   and its first word is yet to be stepped
  2E6A'   0000                  OPSTA:	DW	0		; operator: span start (cursor before the motion)
  2E6C'   0000                  OPEND2: DW	0		; operator: span end (cursor after the motion)
  2E6E'   00 00 00              OPSFP:	DB	0,0,0		; operator: the start as a place in the file
  2E71'   00 00 00              OPSLO:	DB	0,0,0		; ... where its line starts
  2E74'   00 00 00              OPSHI:	DB	0,0,0		; ... where the next line does (OPPGD: the span's
                                				;   high end, whichever line that turned out to be)
  2E77'   00 00 00              OPTOGO: DB	0,0,0		; OPPGD: bytes still to take
  2E7A'   0000                  OPLO:	DW	0		; operator: low offset of the delete range
  2E7C'   0000                  OPHI:	DW	0		; operator: high offset of the delete range
                                
  2E7E'   0000                  CNTV:	DW	0		; motion repeat counter
  2E80'   0000                  TTI:	DW	0		; TAILTAB scan index
  2E82'   0000                  WKI:	DW	0		; scan index
  2E84'   0000                  WKTERM: DW	0		; line terminator offset
  2E86'   0000                  WKBOL:	DW	0		; EOLC scratch: line BOL
  2E88'   00                    WKCLS:	DB	0		; word motion: class of the current run
  2E89'   00                    VRCH:	DB	0		; 'r' / R: the char being stored
  2E8A'   0000                  RPDEL:	DW	0		; R: chars overwritten, still in the gap (BS puts back)
  2E8C'   0000                  RPAPP:	DW	0		; R: chars appended past the line end (BS deletes)
  2E8E'   0000                  RPGE:	DW	0		; R: GAPEND as the last overwrite left it
                                
                                
  2E90'   0000                  RLPTR:	DW	0		; RDLINE: write pointer
  2E92'   00                    RLLEN:	DB	0		; RDLINE: chars stored
  2E93'   00                    RLMAX:	DB	0		; RDLINE: capacity
  2E94'   00                    EXOP:	DB	0		; ex: the command (EO_xx)
  2E95'   00                    EXARG:	DB	0		; ex: 0 = no file name given
  2E96'   00                    EXBAK:	DB	0		; ex: the write keeps a backup
  2E97'   0000                  EXCMD:	DW	0		; ex: text shown after a message
  2E99'   00                    WPROW:	DB	0		; :w -- the cursor's row
  2E9A'   00                    WPBYT:	DB	0		; :w -- the cursor's byte in its record
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-144


  2E9B'   0000                  WPREC:	DW	0		; :w -- the cursor's record in the file
  2E9D'   0000                  ZDIF:	DW	0		; G/gg: target - top (low 16 bits)
  2E9F'   00                    ZLF:	DB	0		; G/gg: lines from the target up to the top
  2EA0'   0000                  ZSVCNT: DW	0		; G/gg: CMDCNT around LINPSB
  2EA2'   0000                  ZSVAUX: DW	0		; G/gg: AUXCNT around LINPSB
  2EA4'   00                    ZSVDIR: DB	0		; G/gg: DIRFLG around LINPSB
  2EA5'   00                    MSGWHT: DB	0		; the message the bottom row still owes (MW_xxx)
  2EA6'   00                    MSGNEW: DB	0		; the file was not there when it was opened ('[New]')
  2EA7'   00                    EXBANG: DB	0		; ex: 0 = '!' given
  2EA8'   00                    ROFLAG: DB	0		; command line: '-R' / '/R' was given
  2EA9'   00                    PLUSF:	DB	0		; command line: '+' was given
  2EAA'   00                    YMODF:	DB	0		; yy: MODF across the delete + put back
  2EAB'   00                    YDCHG:	DB	0		; yy: DOTCHG across it too (see '.')
  2EAC'   00                    YURPL:	DB	0		; yy: UNDRPL across it too (see 'u')
  2EAD'   0000                  YOFF:	DW	0		; yy: the cursor, which a yank leaves alone
  2EAF'   0000                  YPT:	DW	0		; yy: where the lines came from
  2EB1'   0000                  PCNT:	DW	0		; p/P: copies still to put
  2EB3'   00 00 00              PPFP:	DB	0,0,0		; p/P: the first line put, a file position
  2EB6'   0000                  PLFS:	DW	0		; p/P: the lines in the register
  2EB8'   00                    PCPY:	DB	0		; p/P: the copies, kept for the paint
  2EB9'   0000                  YLDLT:	DW	0		; yy: LNDLT across the delete + put back
                                
  2EBB'   00                    SDIR:	DB	0		; the last '/' or '?' direction (FFH = back)
  2EBC'   00                    SDIRE:	DB	0		; ... the direction THIS search runs in ('N')
  2EBD'   00                    SHAVE:	DB	0		; nonzero once a pattern has been entered
  2EBE'   00                    FNDKND: DB	0		; f/F/t/T: which find ';' repeats (0 = none yet)
  2EBF'   00                    FNDCH:	DB	0		; ... and the character it looked for
  2EC0'   00                    FNDRUN: DB	0		; the find THIS command is running (',' reverses)
  2EC1'   00                    FNDSTP: DB	0		; how far along it starts: 2 for a 't' repeat
  2EC2'   0000                  FNDN:	DW	0		; occurrences still to count off
  2EC4'   0000                  FNDCUR: DW	0		; the cursor it started from
  2EC6'   0000                  FNDLIM: DW	0		; ... and the end of the line it may not pass
  2EC8'   00                    GPKEEP: DB	0		; GPLACE: keep the column (a search) not FNBLNK
  2EC9'   00 00 00              MKVAL:	DB	0,0,0		; marks: nonzero once 'm' has set that one.  It
                                				;   lives HERE and not in MKTAB because the image
                                				;   guarantees a DB, and nothing guarantees a DS
  2ECC'   00 00 00              MKEDIT: DB	0,0,0		; MKSHFT: where in the file the edit is
  2ECF'   0000                  MKN:	DW	0		; ... how long it is
  2ED1'   00                    MKTYP:	DB	0		; ... and which way: 1 text went in, 2 came out
  2ED2'   00 00 00              MKPOS:	DB	0,0,0		; MKJMP: the file position being jumped to
  2ED5'   00                    MKWHAT: DB	0		; which of 'm' / '`' / ''' is running
  2ED6'   00                    MKHOLD: DB	0		; set across YKGO's delete + put back (a yank
                                				;   that could not copy): MKSHFT must leave the
                                				;   marks alone through it
  2ED7'   0000                  YACT:	DW	0		; how this yank takes its lines (VD_DD / YSPAN)
  2ED9'   0000                  YTGT:	DW	0		; YSPAN: the text's length once the span is out
  2EDB'   0000                  YWAS:	DW	0		; ... and its length BEFORE the lines came out
  2EDD'   0000                  YCOL:	DW	0		; ... how far into them the cursor sat
  2EDF'   00 00 00              YPFP:	DB	0,0,0		; ... and where in the file they start
                                ;--- the yank that copies instead (YNCOPY / YNSPAN) ---
  2EE2'   0000                  YNPG:	DW	0		; the window the yank started in (YNDONE's paint)
  2EE4'   0000                  YNCNT:	DW	0		; YNLINE: the lines it still wants
  2EE6'   0000                  SCNT:	DW	0		; searches still to do (the count)
  2EE8'   00 00 00              SOFF:	DB	0,0,0		; BRKON: where in the FILE the command began
  2EEB'   0000                  SLDLT:	DW	0		; ... and LNDLT: for a search that fails, and
                                				;   any move an ESC stops
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-145


  2EED'   00                    BRKARM: DB	0		; nonzero: CNTTST looks for an ESC
  2EEE'   00                    BRKHIT: DB	0		; nonzero: one was found, and the count spent
  2EEF'   00                    SSKIP:	DB	0		; skip the character under the cursor (not
                                				;   on the wrapped pass)
                                
  0024                          SREPMX	EQU	EXMAX-4		; ':s' -- bounded by the ex line itself, so
                                				;   SCOPY can never truncate a line anyone is
                                				;   able to type: the shortest possible
                                				;   substitute is 's' '/' + one pattern char +
                                				;   '/' before the replacement starts, and
                                				;   RDLINE stores at most EXMAX.  Tied to
                                				;   EXMAX rather than written out, the way
                                				;   BUF.MAC ties SRCMAX to SRMAX, so the two
                                				;   cannot drift apart.
  2EF0'   00                    SRNGF:	DB	0		; ex: a line range was given
  2EF1'   0000                  SRNG1:	DW	0		; ex: its first line
  2EF3'   0000                  SRNG2:	DW	0		; ... and its last (FFFFH = the file's last)
  2EF5'   00                    SDELIM: DB	0		; ':s' the delimiter typed
  2EF6'   00                    SGFLAG: DB	0		; ':s' the 'g' flag
  2EF7'   00                    SANY:	DB	0		; ':s' something in the range has changed
  2EF8'   00                    SLCHG:	DB	0		; ':s' this line changed
  2EF9'   0000                  SBACK:	DW	0		; ':s' lines walked past the last change
  2EFB'   0000                  SLINES: DW	0		; ':s' / ':N,Mw' lines of the range still to do
  2EFD'   00                    RWPTR:	DB	0		; ':N,Mw' the next free byte of the record at
                                				;   0080H, as that address's low byte
  2EFE'   00                    RWERR:	DB	0		; ':N,Mw' a record would not go: the disk is full
  2EFF'   00                    RDANY:	DB	0		; ':r' something has been read in
  2F00'   00                    RDBRK:	DB	0		; ':r' the line above wants a line end first
  2F01'   00 00 00              SOFFS:	DB	0,0,0		; ':s' the cursor's place in the file, for one that
  2F04'   0000                  SLDLTS: DW	0		;   changes nothing, and its LNDLT
  2F06'   00                    SREPLN: DB	0		; ':s' the replacement's length, kept
  2F07'                         SREPL:	DS	SREPMX+1	;   just below it: WM's RDSTR layout,
                                				;   so INSSTR can splice it
                                
                                	END
